cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Interflect: deterministic surfel radiosity on signed distance fields"
message: "If you use this software or the approach it describes, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - alias: arunvpp
    email: arunvpp24@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/Cherie05/interflect"
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-09-02"
keywords:
  - global illumination
  - radiosity
  - signed distance fields
  - deterministic rendering
  - CPU rendering
abstract: >-
  A noise-free CPU renderer. Classical radiosity produced converged,
  view-independent global illumination but required surfaces subdivided into
  well-conditioned patches, and fragile automatic meshing is why it lost to
  path tracing. A signed distance field has no mesh to subdivide: any point in
  space projects onto the surface by Newton iteration along the gradient, so
  patches can be generated at any density with no topology and no failure
  cases. Interflect combines meshless surfel placement on an SDF with a
  classical radiosity solve over a sparse form-factor matrix, giving global
  illumination with no stochastic sampling and therefore no image noise and no
  denoiser. Output is bit-identical across thread counts and runs.
